## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of non-trivial bit rearrangements across 128 bits without loops. The task blends endian concepts with mask-shift patterns, common in low-level data handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit data as hi/lo halves
- Byte-wise reversal using masks and shifts
- Endianness-aware data transformations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline (actual C-like code uses uint128 struct)
static uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t t = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  t = ((t & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((t & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  return (t << 32) | (t >> 32);
}
uint128 endianSwap128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = bswap64(x.lo); r.lo = bswap64(x.hi); return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable endianness at compile time? 
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?
